course: dictionary
lesson: 8866
title: Shore: land by the water, or to hold up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A shore is the land at the edge of a sea or lake, where the land meets the water. «Swim near the shore and between the flags» tells you to stay close to land, where the lifeguards can see you.
explain: Shore is also used as a verb, to shore something up. It means to hold something up with supports so it does not fall. Spelling is the same, so use the context to choose. Is there water nearby? Then it is probably the land. Is someone holding something up? Then it is the verb.
explain: «Going South, with the rest of them,» he murmured, «Seawards first and then on shipboard, and so to the shores that are calling me!» Here shores means faraway lands by the sea, which you can tell from the ship and the sea. Always ask what word would fit, then test your meaning in the sentence.
fact: The shore is the land at the edge of a sea or lake.
fact: «The seashore» is the shore by the sea. «Offshore» means out from the shore, in the water.
fact: «Ashore» means onto the land, away from the water, as in «to swim ashore».
fact: To shore up something means to hold it up with supports so it does not fall.
fact: Waves and tides wear away the shore, and crabs and seabirds live there.
exercise: «Shells were found on the shore.» Where were the shells found? | Inside a castle | In a shop | Under a bed | *On the land at the edge of the sea
exercise: The safety sign says, «Swim near the shore and between the flags.» Where should you swim? | In the middle of the sea, far from the flags | *Close to the shore, between the flags | Under the pier | Out past the rocks
exercise: «The workers had to shore up the wall before it fell.» What does shore up mean? | To paint it bright blue | To knock it down | To wash it clean | *To hold it up with supports so it does not fall
test: «Waves wear away the shore.» In this sentence, what is the shore? | *The land at the edge of the sea or lake | The bottom of the ocean | A boat that sails on the sea | A kind of wave
test: «To the shores that are calling me!» What does shores mean here? | Dreams that wake you up | Tall, steep cliffs | *Faraway lands by the sea | Small waves that roll in
exam: If a rip current carries you out, which way should you swim? | Straight back at the shore, against the current | Down deep, to the bottom of the sea | Away from the shore, as fast as you can | *Parallel to the shore, until you are out of the rip
